## Why This Is Asked
This question probes understanding of cache invalidation, versioned keys, outbox patterns, and end-to-end consistency in CNPA stacks. It also checks how to observe impact and plan safe rollouts.

## Key Concepts
- Cache invalidation strategies and TTLs
- Redis key versioning and cache-aside pattern
- Event-driven invalidation via Kafka
- Safe reads with read-fallbacks and refresh
- Observability of latency and hit ratios

## Code Example
```javascript
// Update path
BEGIN;
UPDATE profiles SET name=$1, email=$2, version = version + 1 WHERE id=$id;
COMMIT;
redis.del(`profile:${id}:v*`);
publishKafka('profile_updated', { id, version: NEW_VERSION });
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test race conditions when read happens concurrently with update?
- How would you tune TTLs and decide when to prune old keys?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of end-to-end exactly-once guarantees across HTTP, DB, Kafka, and warehouse sinks. Emphasizes practical patterns (transactional outbox, Kafka transactions) and robust dedup in the sink.

## Key Concepts

- transactional outbox pattern
- Kafka transactions for exactly-once publish
- idempotent sink with dedup window
- dedup store and watermarking for late arrivals
- end-to-end validation under burst

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: transactional outbox and dedup
BEGIN;
INSERT INTO users (...) VALUES (...);
INSERT INTO outbox (user_id, event_id, payload) VALUES (...);
COMMIT;

// Sink path (idempotent)
const key = `${user_id}:${event_id}`;
if (dedup.has(key)) return; // skip duplicates
dedup.add(key);
WRITE_WAREHOUSE(payload);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test for out-of-order delivery and still maintain exactly-once semantics?
- What metrics signal dedup failures, and how would you roll back safely?
- How would you handle schema evolution if event payload changes in the outbox?

